Z||000006||87GTZ34||06-20-2001||04:09 PM||||Alex,  I didn't look up the post but there was someone else that previously had/or has this problem.  My car also does it.  I have changed the same stuff you have and the dist was replaced not too long ago by the previous owner. It looks new & I've only put 800 miles on the car since I got it.  Mine has no problem under acceleration, only with very little load will it sputter.  I noticed when I did my heads that the cam chain is very loose.  I have the parts to fix it but haven't put them on yet. I was also thinking injectors but the only thing with that theory is that it is pretty strong under acceleration.  I'll hit this thread when I put the chain & sprockets on. GL||||reg||1
Z||000007||ROB MAC||06-20-2001||04:31 PM||||I have that problem and recently had code 42: ignition module. I have been told that bench testing isn't 100% acurate. Am going to replace and see if all problems vanish.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Steven||06-20-2001||04:32 PM||||My first guess would be to look at your distributor. Pop the cap off and look at the pick up fingers. Are the rust covered, is the pick up itself still in good shape. The reason I say the distributor is that My 85 GT seems to like a new one every 60-65 thousand miles. I did just buy an after market dis. for the first time (orginal was replaced at 63,000 at dealership. dealership replaced at engine rebuild 128000 with aftermarket). If has a different design. Instead of stationary pick of fingers sending a signal to the fingers on the shaft. This one has a round stationary with 6 points on the ID, and a round piece on the shaft that has six points. It was an A-1 Cardone bought at Advanced Auto here in Michigan. I don't know if they are a national chain of stores or just a state wide chain. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT<BR>3.2 liter w/4sp manual trans<BR>Silver.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||AkursedX||06-20-2001||06:17 PM||||You know, now that I think about it.....I think my cap and rotor are about a year old now.....I better check them.  Hope that's the problem.<P>But one more thing.  I replaced my oxygen sensor about a week ago, but I forgot to unplug the computer.  So I finally did it a couple days ago, but it was still missing.  That's why I was thinking is was maybe some sort of fuel issue, maybe running lean.  <P>But I went on a nice 40 mile ride today, and when I got home, I checked to see if it was still "pooting"  and I didn't really hear any.  So I'm hoping maybe the computer has finally corrected itself.  ||||reg||3
Z||000010||AkursedX||06-22-2001||09:56 PM||||Just as an update, I took off my cap and rotor, and they looked like crap.  That's what I get for running an upgraded ignition system with a crappy, and old cap and rotor.<P>So I went and bought a new upgraded cap and rotor, and it runs a heck of alot better now!<P>But....I still do have a slight miss  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>But it's alot better!<P>So I conclude my problem is somewhere in my ignition system.  My next step is to check if i have any fouled plugs.<P>Although I still think it could be a plugged injector...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
